 ## Send MIDI Beat Clock to external hardware
 
-On Windows, macOS or Linux, open **Setup › MIDI › MIDI Beat Clock…**. Choose the MIDI output connected to the receiving device, check **Enable MIDI Beat Clock**, and click **OK**. Set the receiving device to follow external MIDI clock, then start SoundCraft playback. The output follows the session tempo at 24 clock pulses per quarter note. Playback from the beginning sends Start. Playback from a later position sends Song Position Pointer and Continue. Stopping playback sends Stop.
+On Windows, macOS or Linux, open **Setup › MIDI › MIDI Beat Clock…**. Choose the MIDI output connected to the receiving device, check **Enable MIDI Beat Clock**, and click **OK**. Set the receiving device to follow external MIDI clock, then start SoundCraft playback. The output follows the session tempo at 24 clock pulses per quarter note. The offset-adjusted position determines the start message. At zero, the receiver gets Start. Starting, seeking or looping between sixteenths sends Song Position Pointer and Continue for the next sixteenth; its first clock arrives on that boundary. Playback before the adjusted origin waits for zero. Stop ends the outgoing pulses. A fresh position beyond 16,383 sixteenths cannot be encoded; clock output resumes after a locate in range. A continuous pass can play beyond that limit.
 
-**Offset (samples)** shifts the outgoing clock relative to session playback. A positive value delays the clock and a negative value advances it. The session saves the enable switch, destination and offset. After connecting a MIDI device, choose **Refresh devices** to update the list. If a saved destination is missing, its name stays selected. Reconnect it or choose another output to enable the clock.
+**Offset (samples)** shifts the outgoing clock relative to session playback. A positive value delays the clock and a negative value advances it. The session saves the enable switch, destination and offset. After connecting a MIDI device, choose **Refresh devices** to update the list. If a saved destination is missing, its name stays selected. Reconnect it or choose another output to enable the clock. Open or send failures appear in the dialog and the worker retries the port. Outputs with identical names require distinct device names before selection can identify one reliably.
 
